Shish <shish@villatatra.plus.com> writes:
> Being on the first level I decided to try on some unknown items,
> figuring I could just restart the game if anything majorly bad
> happened;

I've no argument with extra risk-taking in the early game, but if
something majorly bad does happen, you may want to consider trying to
deal with it anyway rather than just #quit-ing. That'll give you
experience in dealing with whatever the major problem is, before you
encounter it in a game in which you've invested a huge amount of time.
(Just as you did with the c[oLev.)

There's also a "slippery slope" argument, along the lines of: "Trying
un-IDed magic items is like drinking out of every fountain you see is
like re-rolling until you get stats you like. Re-rolling is cheap, so
don't do any of these things." But I'm not the one to make it.[1]

I will suggest that you at least BUC-ID stuff before trying it.
Taking early-game risks is interesting; taking trivially-avoidable
ones, less so.

> "You cannot reach the floor" - but then on finding a statue, which I
> would think I could grab the top of and drag myself down, I'm also
> told that I can't get to the floor :/

I hereby nominate this as Yet Another Can of Worms, in that it brings
into question issues of scale which are not easily reconcilable with
the basic nethack mechanics. (Things like dragons and mumakil take up
a single square each, as do ants, mold and hobbits.)

You can hand-wave away the problem you describe with something like:
The ceilings are high -- say, 30' -- and statues are all human-scale
(5-6') rather than life-size. If you find a statue of a mastodon and
it comes to life, that process includes an increase in size.

In regards your cursed boots of levitation: Others upthread have made
good and useful suggestions regarding prayer and sinks. These are the
most likely to be do-able and the least likely to have nasty
side-effects.  To a much lesser extent, the "nymph" one is likewise.

I'd commend this to you as a lesson in the meta-principle that, in
nethack, there are often many solutions to a particular problem, and
that it profits you to know as many as you can, in the hopes of
finding the one with the best cost/benefit in your particular
situation.

In addition to the suggestions others have made, I'd suggest also:
Poly (by trap, potion, ring, wand, chameleon corpse or tin of ditto),
scrolls of remove curse or enchant armor, or potions of holy water.
(As an aside, eating of mimic does _not_ work; it creates only an
urge to _act_ mimicish, not an actual shape-change.)

